# std::convert — `From` and `into`.
#
# Two traits, and the observation that they are one relation written from two
# ends: a `Meters` that can be made *from* an `int` is an `int` that goes
# *into* a `Meters`. The blanket impl below is what makes that literally true
# rather than merely suggestive, so a program writes `From` and gets `into`
# free — and never writes `into` at all.
#
# That direction is not a style preference, it is what the machinery allows. An
# impl is selected by a (self type, trait reference) pair, and a conversion is
# exactly the case where the *receiver does not determine the answer*: `7.into()`
# could produce anything at all. So `into` needs the expected type to pin the
# impl, while `from` is qualified by the type it produces and needs nothing.
# Writing the trait whose self type is the *output* is what puts the choice
# where a name can be written down.
#
# **The blanket costs a program the ability to write its own `into` impl.**
# Coherence is blind to an impl's bounds, so `impl<T, U: From<T>> into<U> for T`
# overlaps every `impl into<X> for Y` that could ever be written — importing
# this module makes writing one an error. That is Rust's rule arrived at from
# the same direction, and it is the price of the free direction being free.
#
# The reflexive `impl<T> From<T> for T` is deliberately **not** here for the
# same reason one level worse: it would overlap every `From` impl too, and then
# the module would ship a trait nobody could implement.
#
# **What it ships is two conversions**, and the restraint is the point: an
# import's cost to its dependents is measured in impls (see `std::cmp`), and a
# conversion trait is the easiest module in the library to overfill. Both are
# lossless and neither is expressible as a method today.
#
# There is deliberately no `From<T> for string`. Rendering a value is
# `std::fmt::Display`'s question and `to_string` already answers it; a
# conversion trait answering it too would give the language two spellings for
# one thing, which is the split milestone 18 drew between `print` and
# `Display` and worth not redrawing here.
#
# Nor is there a `From<float> for int`: `as int` truncates, and a conversion
# that silently loses the fraction is not one a `From` should hide. Which way
# it should round is a question a fallible conversion would have to ask, and
# there is no `TryFrom` yet.

# Imported as a module, not by item: `code` is a method now (milestone 40), so
# the `use` is only here to make `impl char` visible for `value.code()` below.
use std::char;

# `Self` is the type produced, which is why `from` is an associated function
# and not a method: there is no receiver until it returns.
pub trait From<T> {
    fun from(value: T) -> Self;
}

pub trait into<T> {
    fun into(self) -> T;
}

# The whole module. `U::from` is an associated function reached through a
# bound — the impl supplying it is not known until `U` is concrete, which is
# the same dispatch a method call on a bounded receiver gets, with the path
# carrying the abstract self type instead of a receiver.
#
# Neither the receiver nor a named trait reference pins `U` at a bare
# `x.into()`; the type the result flows into does.
impl<T, U: From<T>> into<U> for T {
    fun into(self) -> U {
        return U::from(self);
    }
}

# Widening, which is the one numeric conversion that keeps its value.
impl From<int> for float {
    fun from(value: int) -> float {
        return value as float;
    }
}

# A character's scalar value. `c.code()` is the native behind it, and the
# dependency costs only `std::char`'s own `char` methods — the one impl it ships,
# and none from what it imports.
impl From<char> for int {
    fun from(value: char) -> int {
        return value.code();
    }
}
